<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayyubid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayyubid dynasty, Ayyubids, Ayyubid Sultanate, Ayyubid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ayyubid Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim dynasty founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1171 and united Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Saladin's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> in 1187 led to the recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>. After his death the realm was divided among family branches, and in 1250 it was overthrown in Egypt by its own mamluk soldiers, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire.jpg" alt="Ayyubid Empire" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayyubid_Sultanate_1193_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Richard I of England’s victory at Arsuf in 1191 helped restore the coast, but another field army was destroyed by the Khwarazmians and <b>Ayyubids</b> at La Forbie in 1244.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with Egypt worsened under the <b>Ayyubids</b>: after a Nubian attack on Aswan, Shams al-Dawla Turan-Shah, brother of Saladin, sacked Qasr Ibrim in 1172–1173.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Shajar al-Durr, al-Salih's widow, briefly ruled as sultan before opposition from the Abbasid caliph and Syrian <b>Ayyubids</b> led to her marriage to the commander Aybak, who became sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Baldwin I of Jerusalem and Sigurd I of Norway took it after a siege and made it the centre of the Lordship of Sidon; Saladin captured it in 1187, German crusaders restored it in 1197, the <b>Ayyubids</b> destroyed it in 1249 and the Mongols under Kitbuqa in 1260.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When Saladin took Amid from the Zengid coalition in April 1183, the <b>Ayyubids</b> counted among its stores 80,000 candles, a tower full of arrowheads and 1,040,000 books.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>During his reign the <b>Ayyubids</b> defeated the Fifth Crusade, and in 1229 he surrendered Jerusalem to the emperor Frederick II under the Treaty of Jaffa.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
